16
00:01:22,789 --> 00:01:29,030
Now in your book modern CLIs you tell a history of the CLI which I found very insightful.

17
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:32,950
I think a good place to start would be what is a CLI?

18
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:45,510
A command line interface is an interface into a computing environment right that is predicated on a terminal and generally a shell and you know the terminal gives access to the computer.

19
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:51,270
The shell enables both scripts and programs.

20
00:01:51,270 --> 00:02:01,030
And these programs or these CLIs are literally built to do discrete pieces of work inside of an operating system, whether it be at the operating system level or with a business application.

21
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:03,030
Give a couple of examples.

22
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,590
Sure.

23
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:13,990
If we date ourselves back to the 70s, the classic command line interfaces of GP, which is a filter for text files, sort, which is an ordering filter.

24
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,790
You've got cat which is a pipe out into standard out type command line.

25
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:23,270
Those are tried andrue age-old command line interfaces.

26
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:30,230
If we come into the modern era, there are command lines such as gh which is the GitHub command line interface counterpart to its guey. Git.

27
00:02:30,239 --> 00:02:36,550
Obviously the WordPress ecosystem features the WP CLI which is a modern command line interface.

28
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:44,150
So you'll find them in all forms and fashion but they do have some commonalities at least the good ones that have survived the test of time.

29
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:50,229
When I picked up your book, I was expecting something more like the modern use cases you gave for CLIs.

30
00:02:50,229 --> 00:02:55,750
I always thought those old school things like GP were commands.

31
00:02:55,750 --> 00:03:02,710
But maybe I'm like that famous character in literature who was surprised to learn he had been speaking pros all his life.

32
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:05,589
Is a command and a command line interface the same thing?

33
00:03:05,599 --> 00:03:07,270
That's a great question, Robert.

34
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:13,830
And the short answer is no. You have commands, right?

35
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:18,390
But the command line interface is the gateway into the computer if you would that would invoke those commands.

36
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:32,229
So historically we went from punch cards as the interface to invoke functionality and commands to a terminal which was a gateway into a shell that enabled access to scripting environments and commands.

37
00:03:32,239 --> 00:03:41,030
And then obviously in the modern world we use graphical gateways such as applications and browser based apps and mobile apps.

38
00:03:41,030 --> 00:03:46,789
I have been in the field probably about as long as you and I see commands in a certain way.

39
00:03:46,789 --> 00:03:50,710
You have an interesting history of the CLI.

40
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:53,110
Tell us that story.

41
00:03:53,110 --> 00:04:02,390
Yeah, so I chunked the history up and purposely in the book did not want to get into the dredges of every little incremental innovation in the terminal and command line history.

42
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,149
But we chunked it up into basically four or five parts.

43
00:04:06,149 --> 00:04:20,150
You know, the first being the early years where they gained momentum as we transitioned from punch cards into an actual teletype interface that allowed users to type into and view their output first through a printer and later on the terminal.

44
00:04:20,150 --> 00:04:28,710
We then saw acceleration in the Unix world as we began to see both terminal as well as shell innovations and you know we got momentum.

45
00:04:28,710 --> 00:04:35,830
The real dark era for the command line was in the 90s when the popularity of the guey really started to rise

46
00:04:35,830 --> 00:04:44,310
and the question was sincerely asked do we really need command line interfaces to a computer can we not do it all through a graphical user interface

47
00:04:44,310 --> 00:04:53,990
and the short answer to that was no which led into the Unix era which was the revival of what had been built in the Unix era and you know brought life again

48
00:04:53,990 --> 00:05:01,909
to command lines and then we started into the modern era to where the innovators who have built modern command line interfaces have really done some amazing things.

49
00:05:01,909 --> 00:05:11,430
So that was the way we chose to look at it and wanted to kind of set some context so that the readers could understand where this came from and what were the major catalysts that caused for the changes that we've seen today.

50
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:18,230
Your story is the rise and fall and rise of command lines.

51
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:20,070
They're back and bigger than ever before.

52
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:23,029
They are and they're back at scale.

53
00:05:23,029 --> 00:05:30,070
So if you think about the command line of the past, you know, the 70s and 80s version, it was used by power users.

54
00:05:30,070 --> 00:05:37,270
It was used by administrators and it was used in what I would call high value environments.

55
00:05:37,270 --> 00:05:45,749
You had one administrator, a few high dollar machines, and it was a very kind of protected access to a machine.

56
00:05:45,749 --> 00:05:53,830
If you fast forward to today, every developer has access to shells, terminals, and they have access to command line tools such as Git,

57
00:05:53,830 --> 00:05:59,350
GH, and for GitHub, Docker, Kubernetes, each of the major cloud providers offers a CLI.

58
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:03,350
These things are really a powering force in our productivity and workflows today.

59
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:04,950
And I think that that's really cool.

60
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:07,110
I have a question in my outline.

61
00:06:07,110 --> 00:06:16,150
We've been talking about this already a little bit, but I don't think you've told us exactly what is modern about modern CLIs.

62
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:17,990
Yeah.

63
00:06:17,990 --> 00:06:19,749
So, there's a couple aspects to that.

64
00:06:19,749 --> 00:06:24,469
I'm going to start with the most basic, which is what we would call the command model.

65
00:06:24,469 --> 00:06:34,230
And if you go back to the early days, the Unix philosophy really stated that these command lines should do one thing really well and play well with others.

66
00:06:34,230 --> 00:06:37,110
So you saw commands that were created for a specific purpose.

67
00:06:37,110 --> 00:06:48,150
If I were to come up with a hypothetical example of like for a CRM application, a simpleton command line would be CRM add, right?

68
00:06:48,150 --> 00:06:53,990
We would add a customer and it might take an arguments and we might have a few switches that would give some additional functionality.

69
00:06:53,990 --> 00:07:02,150
Well, with that mentality design-wise, you'd also have to have a CRM delete, a CRM change, a CRM list,

70
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:09,110
and you can see where the duplicity in the code and the management of that becomes a real problem very quickly.

71
00:07:09,110 --> 00:07:20,309
So, in that traditional world, you saw the command line evolve to have arguments and then a more organized set of switches that would allow a single CLI to do multiple things.

72
00:07:20,309 --> 00:07:28,309
So, put that Robert in a bucket is the traditional When Lennis Torbolt introduced Git into the ecosystem,

73
00:07:28,309 --> 00:07:35,670
he took some best practices out of a few different worlds and introduced what we now refer to as the object command model.

74
00:07:35,670 --> 00:07:44,550
And it is literally a command and subcommand model that is usually organized around either a noun verb or a verb noun.

75
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:51,670
So if I were to take for example the WP CLI, which is WordPress's command line interface, everything is organized around the noun,

76
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:57,990
a plug-in, a theme, core, and then it's followed by a subcomand that's a verb.

77
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:11,670
The reason why this is so innovative is it allowed very complex command line interfaces to be built and have the code isolated almost like we would a micros service in a SAS environment.

78
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:17,430
and it allowed reuse of centralized code and global flags

79
00:08:17,430 --> 00:08:29,029
and just really really robust organization of the application and that is the preferred model today is the object command model even though there's still modern CLIs that are written traditionally that's what we see today so that's one aspect of it

80
00:08:29,039 --> 00:08:38,230
if I could think of an example of the traditional the old Linux admin commands user ad user mod

81
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:47,990
I forget if there's a user Dell now if that were written today would be one command user and there would be subcommands add remove edit.

82
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,389
Did I get that right?

83
00:08:50,389 --> 00:08:57,910
There would so I would almost organize that traditionally and say that that user right and then the argument could be a username

84
00:08:57,910 --> 00:09:06,630
or an ID and we would have switches that would be like an action equals add delete or we could have a dash- add a d-delete.

85
00:09:06,630 --> 00:09:14,070
If you were to think about the Unix operating system or the Linux operating system holistically,

86
00:09:14,070 --> 00:09:23,190
you might have a general command like we do with with MySQL to where you're going to have objects inside of it, which could be nouns, and then verbs that follow.

87
00:09:23,190 --> 00:09:25,750
So, an object could be a user, add.

88
00:09:25,750 --> 00:09:30,630
An object could be a package, add or delete.

89
00:09:30,630 --> 00:09:33,030
An object could be a networking protocol, add or delete.

90
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:45,590
And so you can see how that would allow a single command line whether it be in a ripple which is a shell like my SQL or strictly at the command line to do a lot more things in a very consistent way.

91
00:09:45,590 --> 00:09:55,110
And the key is we got help out of the man pages and got them into the command line so that the user can actually see all of the options

92
00:09:55,110 --> 00:10:00,790
and configurations that are available to them without having to bounce in and out of of man pages or other documentation.

93
00:10:00,790 --> 00:10:07,990
So what you're talking about now the builtin selfdocumenting feature of most modern CLIs.

94
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,951
Yes. Yes. And that was significant.

95
00:10:10,951 --> 00:10:22,550
It once again had been introduced in bits and pieces but tools really popularized that with git as we brought all of that into the CLI itself.

96
00:10:22,550 --> 00:10:31,590
You mentioned git a few times and in your book you state that it was highly innovative in the CLI space.

97
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:36,870
What were some of the great innovations that git brought the CLI world?

98
00:10:36,870 --> 00:10:41,590
So first of all, if you think about scale,

99
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:50,630
the original CIL mantra was with inside of an operating system and it might venture its way something such as arsync to where it's going to communicate between another machine.

100
00:10:50,630 --> 00:10:52,870
But when you look at Git,

101
00:10:52,870 --> 00:11:06,069
we are talking about local and remote connectivity and the ability to manage both sides of the repository equation at scale across hundreds of millions of users.

102
00:11:06,069 --> 00:11:12,790
It really thought this out in a way that though people will run into their challenges with their repo,

103
00:11:12,790 --> 00:11:19,430
you don't see the challenges of repository management of the past to where code is lost and documents are, you know, overwritten.

104
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:22,870
you know, they really got that right at scale.

105
00:11:22,870 --> 00:11:29,509
And I think that that was a very modern forethought on behalf of the designers and and the maintainers today is to make this work at scale.

106
00:11:29,519 --> 00:11:38,710
Something that I think of as the modern CLI is a command line tool for calling an API.

107
00:11:38,710 --> 00:11:41,191
You call this the API first architecture. What do you mean by that?

108
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:51,910
In distributed computing, right, we have gravitated from using SDK functional calls locally to calling APIs.

109
00:11:51,910 --> 00:12:05,590
So the modern command line interface finds itself more organized around making these remote calls versus calling an operating system function or versus calling a function from a local package that might be compiled into it.

110
00:12:05,590 --> 00:12:11,430
though that's still fair game for the function of a CLI.

111
00:12:11,430 --> 00:12:16,629
We see a lot more remote communication with APIs in kind of a more modern sense.

112
00:12:16,629 --> 00:12:25,590
And to be honest, any CLI designer really needs to think about leveraging once again the Unix philosophy, do something well and play well with others.

113
00:12:25,590 --> 00:12:35,910
And part of that play well with others has extended from standard in, standard out to being able to make an API call out and receive data back in as a part of that workstream. if that makes sense.

114
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:39,110
I think so.

115
00:12:39,110 --> 00:12:44,389
I think that your point is we're not just pushing text around through a pipe on the Linux file system,

116
00:12:44,399 --> 00:12:51,671
but we're going out over networks and consuming worked services in the same pipelines. Is that where you were going?

117
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:57,590
That's exactly the point I was trying to make.

118
00:12:57,590 --> 00:13:06,949
Suppose I have a great API that I set up for my customers or users and then there's an SDK and now I want to add a CLI.

119
00:13:06,949 --> 00:13:11,750
Should the CLI simply mirror the API structure or do you think about it as a different use case?

120
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:17,110
I hate to give an answer like this, but it does depend.

121
00:13:17,110 --> 00:13:27,110
I would go back to classic UX mantras of what is the persona you're trying to serve and what is the purpose of the CLI. So CLIs can be used for a number of things.

122
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:31,190
Traditionally they've been used for administrative tasks.

123
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:33,190
They've been used for power users.

124
00:13:33,190 --> 00:13:35,670
But I'll give an example.

125
00:13:35,670 --> 00:13:39,509
I'll pick on the WordPress CLI again.

126
00:13:39,509 --> 00:13:46,069
WordPress itself as a PHP and JavaScript primarily driven user interface had challenges with longer running tasks.

127
00:13:46,069 --> 00:13:51,110
So things such as regenerating images on a site was a real problem.

128
00:13:51,110 --> 00:13:57,350
And the WordPress CLI specifically leverages that infrastructure with inside of WordPress core,

129
00:13:57,350 --> 00:14:10,070
but they made their own decisions in terms of performance to be able to accomplish the task and not be victim of some of the shortfalls that the graphical user interface implementation would have and they crushed it. Right?

130
00:14:10,079 --> 00:14:20,230
That's the de facto way to go regenerate images on an entire site is through the CLI because they did it in their way to solve the need for the persona that they were servicing.

131
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:23,509
You just now mentioned longunning tasks.

132
00:14:23,519 --> 00:14:25,750
Something I wanted to ask you about.

133
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:31,350
What is the model or interaction models for CLIs to handle longunning tasks?

134
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:36,870
It depends on what the longunning task looks like.

135
00:14:36,870 --> 00:14:45,910
So first of all when we talk about interacting with APIs one thing that is a headwind in any type of of SAS environments is

136
00:14:45,910 --> 00:15:00,150
if you look at your edge providers or you look at your global traffic managers or even local traffic managers they are not in the business of holding open TCP connections for 5 6 7 10 minutes right it's a real problem it's a resource problem that they manage

137
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:08,710
to so if we think about those task of let's let's take about turning through data.

138
00:15:08,710 --> 00:15:12,710
If we've got a data set to process that is 100 gig,

139
00:15:12,710 --> 00:15:18,710
500 gig, a terabyte, and literally has tens of millions of rows,

140
00:15:18,710 --> 00:15:31,430
that long run running task needs to be mindfully thought out in terms of how we chunk that up and what are the system level programming resources that we leverage.

141
00:15:31,430 --> 00:15:39,030
So in that file example, if I were to look at that and I was sitting on top of a four core or an 8 core machine,

142
00:15:39,030 --> 00:15:48,310
as a CLI designer, my first thought is how do I divide that file up into eight parts and concurrently run a process on that to leverage that multi-core machine.

143
00:15:48,310 --> 00:15:55,829
The second is how do I efficiently leverage the memory that I have locally in a way that I'm not thrashing on the disk.

144
00:15:55,829 --> 00:16:05,030
In the C++ and in the Go world, the map type, right, which is kind of a hash bucket element in memory, has really evolved in recent years.

145
00:16:05,030 --> 00:16:13,350
And Google's patent on what's called a Swiss table, which allows for an 8K descriptor on the bucket allows you to really,

146
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:19,749
really leverage the memory that's available on local machines and use that to speed up process.

147
00:16:19,749 --> 00:16:25,030
So, back to your question of what does a longunning process look like in a CLI?

148
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:29,749
First of all, you want to shorten it as much as possible in a responsible manner.

149
00:16:29,749 --> 00:16:36,310
And then to the extent that it's going to have to continue to be longer or run over periods of time that might be subject to interruptions or failures,

150
00:16:36,310 --> 00:16:47,509
you're going to want to go back to tried and trueue design tactics such as is having break points, recovery points, being able to do retries and not lose that work that has been in process.

151
00:16:47,509 --> 00:16:49,590
So I always go back to my friend Arsync.

152
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:56,389
I cannot tell you how many just challenging situations our sync has has gotten me and and co-workers of mine out of.

153
00:16:56,389 --> 00:17:04,870
And that's a good one to go look at to say if we're going to run something for a day, two days, a week, boy, it sure is nice if you come back to it and something went wrong to be able to start where you left off.

154
00:17:04,880 --> 00:17:10,069
All of what you said certainly very relevant.

155
00:17:10,069 --> 00:17:13,029
Not the question I had in mind.

156
00:17:13,029 --> 00:17:21,029
So let me ask you a different question which is what about a task which it doesn't necessarily involve a staggering amount of data

157
00:17:21,029 --> 00:17:28,711
but I kick it off and it's going to take the provider 2 hours to do. What is a model for that?

158
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:37,110
First you're going to have to be in a in a resource environment that will allow you to do that.

159
00:17:37,110 --> 00:17:45,350
So if you are simply waiting for the provider, let's say that we put a call out that says I need X to be done and that could be,

160
00:17:45,350 --> 00:17:49,830
you know, chinning up a bunch of clusters in a cloud environment.

161
00:17:49,830 --> 00:17:57,990
You're going to want to wait, right, in a responsible way, in a nonblocking way for that command to execute.

162
00:17:57,990 --> 00:18:05,029
And you're going to want to be able to make some decisions along the way as to whether you think that process is ever going to complete.

163
00:18:05,029 --> 00:18:13,029
So in the go world, this would be a combination of spinning up a go routine and associating that with a weight group.

164
00:18:13,029 --> 00:18:20,549
And so let's double down on your question and say what happens if we asked something to do a hundred things and needed all of those to come back.

165
00:18:20,549 --> 00:18:31,830
We could have those assigned to 10 go routines to 10 weight groups and one by one be able to check those off as they completed and maintain a state of waiting until we finish that.

166
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:38,950
And we'd also mindfully need to make some decisions if we thought something was going to error out or not happen to be able to restart it or retry it.

167
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:47,270
You mentioned Go just now, which ties into something else I want to ask you about.

168
00:18:47,270 --> 00:18:51,909
I'm pretty sure the Linux commands we talked about were all written in C.

169
00:18:51,909 --> 00:18:53,750
You're talking about Go as another choice.

170
00:18:53,750 --> 00:18:59,590
What are the popular implementation languages in the more modern space?

171
00:18:59,590 --> 00:19:03,029
So you are still seeing modern CLIs written in C and C++.

172
00:19:03,029 --> 00:19:11,029
In the book we have a spotlight section where we spotlight a handful of really largecale popular CLIs

173
00:19:11,029 --> 00:19:18,549
and two of them one represents C which is get and the other represents C++ which is UCLA speed test

174
00:19:18,549 --> 00:19:26,710
which is a really nifty CLI but you're also seeing CLI written in PHP, Python, Node, C.

175
00:19:26,710 --> 00:19:32,950
All of those have some challenges with them because of run times and the overall plumbing that surrounds them.

176
00:19:32,950 --> 00:19:39,029
But the new kids on the block of of Go and Rust are gaining popularity.

177
00:19:39,029 --> 00:19:48,390
GitHub is an example that GH was written not only in Go, but uses a package called Cobra, which is an object command framework for Go.

178
00:19:48,390 --> 00:19:50,310
And we also highlight a terminal.

179
00:19:50,310 --> 00:19:54,230
It's not a CLI, but it's a it's a smart terminal called warp that was written in Rust.

180
00:19:54,230 --> 00:19:58,230
In my view,

181
00:19:58,230 --> 00:20:11,830
the more you can leverage system programming power in a language but not have to manage the overhead of multi-threading and memory management like we had to traditionally with C and C++.

182
00:20:11,830 --> 00:20:18,950
And the lighter weight the runtime and footprint is, the better of an application that programming language is going to be.

183
00:20:18,950 --> 00:20:21,669
And I am certainly a fan of Go from that perspective.

184
00:20:21,669 --> 00:20:29,430
give a huge nod to Rust, but I've also been active in the WP CLI ecosystem where, you know, PHP is the language of choice.

185
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:37,430
Derek, you just now mentioned Go and some of the tooling that exists to support building CLIs in Go.

186
00:20:37,430 --> 00:20:46,870
Talk about some of the packages in the Go ecosystem specifically that help people to build CLIs.

187
00:20:46,870 --> 00:20:54,070
So, Go as you know has a package ecosystem that supplies functionality and capabilities beyond what Go's core is which is is pretty basic.

188
00:20:54,070 --> 00:21:03,190
