So it really [clears throat] depends on what the workload is in the AWS environment.

378
00:41:26,630 --> 00:41:29,670
You're in there, right, to do work.

379
00:41:29,670 --> 00:41:38,309
And I think that that is the overall premise of allowing a user to get work done before they exit is a good thing

380
00:41:38,309 --> 00:41:45,510
to where in curl you're in and out of that transaction whether you're downloading a web page, you're actually hitting an API because curl can do a lot of things.

381
00:41:45,510 --> 00:41:47,589
It's more transactional in nature.

382
00:41:47,589 --> 00:41:50,069
I think it does present a problem and I come back to SSH.

383
00:41:50,069 --> 00:42:03,511
The way that SSH solved this problem is through public and private keys and I think we are going to continue to want to sharpen the pencil in terms of of how we look at authentication in state management and CLI. There's no one answer.

384
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:08,870
There's one last topic want to hit before we start to wrap up.

385
00:42:08,870 --> 00:42:14,950
I don't know if it's a CLI feature or a shell feature.

386
00:42:14,950 --> 00:42:17,430
How does completion work and should your CLI provide it?

387
00:42:17,440 --> 00:42:30,309
So autocomp completion is literally a file that you would supply with a CLI that would allow the shell environment, right, to be able to read that and autocomplete.

388
00:42:30,309 --> 00:42:32,150
And I believe that you should for two reasons.

389
00:42:32,150 --> 00:42:34,630
one, it's helpful to the end user,

390
00:42:34,630 --> 00:42:43,750
but second, back to this LLM world to where the more data we provide to external entities such as an LLM,

391
00:42:43,750 --> 00:42:47,829
the better you're going to play well in this new world that we're entering into.

392
00:42:47,829 --> 00:42:55,670
It's another reason why I like packages like Cobra that Cobra makes that autocomp completion, you know,

393
00:42:55,670 --> 00:43:03,109
it bakes it in as a package or as a command to your CLI so that you can run it, create those text files with the autocomplete and and you're off to the races.

394
00:43:03,109 --> 00:43:06,309
So, it really reduces the workload on a developer to have to implement that.

395
00:43:06,319 --> 00:43:17,510
We may have talked about this already, but do you have any thoughts that you haven't already expressed on where are CLI's headed?

396
00:43:17,510 --> 00:43:20,309
We've talked about in the LLM driven world,

397
00:43:20,309 --> 00:43:29,589
I think that there is going to be a requirement of CLIs to handle non-deterministic returns in addition to their the current world, which is generally deterministic.

398
00:43:29,589 --> 00:43:36,230
And we're already starting to see those challenges surface in cloud code, for example.

399
00:43:36,230 --> 00:43:42,390
But any CLI that is hitting an LLM and processing structured output, the answer is not going to be the same for every call.

400
00:43:42,390 --> 00:43:47,349
And you're going to have to think through that from a design perspective.

401
00:43:47,349 --> 00:43:52,550
I also think that if we look at the premise of a CLI to go do something, right?

402
00:43:52,550 --> 00:43:58,230
The what and the developer builds in how we do that in a regulatory compliant world.

403
00:43:58,230 --> 00:44:04,470
So thinking healthcare, thinking banking, thinking a number of worlds, even just with a general sock audit,

404
00:44:04,470 --> 00:44:11,670
you also are going to have to be able to capture in the CLI who did it, when they did it, and more importantly, who authorized it.

405
00:44:11,670 --> 00:44:14,309
So now we're getting into workflows.

406
00:44:14,309 --> 00:44:21,430
And those logs might not be punched out into standard error and into your general logging format.

407
00:44:21,430 --> 00:44:34,950
Those logs might require the CLI designer to provide options out to Splunk or Logly or Data Dog or other places where you can punch an asynchronous JSON payload to actually capture that data.

408
00:44:34,950 --> 00:44:39,510
So again, more complications the more complicated our world becomes.

409
00:44:39,510 --> 00:44:49,510
And I think that that is why CLI designers really need to to do upfront design work to capture everything that they need to be thinking about in their CLI.

410
00:44:49,510 --> 00:44:58,150
The last response I would have to that question is if you think about the power that PowerShell has brought to the computing environments.

411
00:44:58,150 --> 00:45:04,790
There's an argument that says you can probably get away with 80% of what you need to do by just writing PowerShell scripts.

412
00:45:04,790 --> 00:45:07,750
And that scares me Robert a little bit. I champion PowerShell.

413
00:45:07,750 --> 00:45:09,589
I champion the people who have gotten good at it.

414
00:45:09,589 --> 00:45:18,870
But I do think there is a still a clean demarcation point between control and workflow which is in the shell environments and the work

415
00:45:18,870 --> 00:45:27,750
to be done and the testing and rigor that needs to go into an actual compiled or let's call it versions in the PHP or Python world application such as a CLI.

416
00:45:27,760 --> 00:45:31,430
Derek, as we wrap up, where can listeners find your book?

417
00:45:31,440 --> 00:45:41,190
Easiest place to get there is on the web at moderncli.dev dev and there are links out to Amazon, Barnes &amp; Noble and and other providers.

418
00:45:41,200 --> 00:45:49,349
It is only available in paperback and hard coverver, but we made pricing super low because I wanted to make it accessible.

419
00:45:49,349 --> 00:45:55,431
Robert, one of the design tenants for the book and one of the reasons why we selected Go for the examples. It's not about Go, but Go.

420
00:45:55,431 --> 00:46:04,150
The examples are in go is I truly wanted whether you could be a high school student or a junior college student and especially if you got a limited budget.

421
00:46:04,160 --> 00:46:15,829
I wanted people to be able to have accessibility to the book and accessibility to the resources they would need to do the exercises in the book either for free or in a very reasonable price point.

422
00:46:15,829 --> 00:46:17,990
And I was really adamant about that as we did this.

423
00:46:17,990 --> 00:46:25,430
Are there any other locations on the web relating to you or your properties you'd like to point listeners to?

424
00:46:25,440 --> 00:46:29,670
Definitely LinkedIn account Derek Schaefer to connect up there on GitHub.

425
00:46:29,680 --> 00:46:39,990
I've actually got a couple of plugins in the WordPress space that have actually been approved and will be coming out and I'm on Twitter, but LinkedIn would be probably the primary place to find me.

426
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:45,589
And my passion and desire to write this book was to capture the history,

427
00:46:45,589 --> 00:46:53,349
the evolution of the CLI and to help people think about how they design them and write the best possible software product

428
00:46:53,359 --> 00:47:02,390
that they can for whatever their use case is, whether it be individually or personally or they're doing something major for their employer in a computing environment.

429
00:47:02,390 --> 00:47:07,990
And I wish people the best of the luck and and congratulate them if they get the opportunity to work on the CLI project because they're quite fun.

430
00:47:08,000 --> 00:47:16,069
Derek, I must say it's been a pleasure speaking to you about modern CLIs and thank you for speaking to Software Engineering Radio.

431
00:47:16,079 --> 00:47:22,309
Robert, thanks for having me. For Software Engineering Radio, this has been Robert Blumen.

432
